=== task: multi medium
title: O(n) with two pointers
prompt: Choose **every** job that two pointers can do in O(n).
* Finding two numbers in a sorted list that add up to a target :: Right. From both ends.
* Merging two sorted lists :: Right. One pointer per list: O(n + m).
* Removing every 0 from a list, in place :: Right. A read pointer and a write pointer.
* Finding the numbers two sorted lists have in common :: Right. One pointer per list, moving the one at the smaller value.
- Finding two numbers that add up to a target in an **unsorted** list, without sorting it or using a set :: The moves need sorted order. Unsorted, you'd sort first or use a set.
- Sorting a list :: Sorting by comparing items needs O(n log n) steps in general.
hint: Which jobs have an order that tells you which pointer to move?
hint: Sorting can't be done in O(n) by comparing items.
right: Pair sums in sorted data, merging, in-place removal and common items all work in one pass. An unsorted pair search needs a set or sorting, and sorting itself is O(n log n).
wrong: Two pointers need a rule for which pointer to move: sorted order, or "keep this item or not". An unsorted pair search has no such rule, and sorting is O(n log n) work on its own.

